A||N||2||Dick McHaffie||More on replacing door locks||||5||
Z||000000||Dick McHaffie||01-12-2000||07:47 PM||||I did an earlier post on this,<P>I got the inside door panel off and located the lock.<P>It appeared that it would be simple.  Just hook my screwdriver on the bent up "fingers" on the top of the clip that holds the back of the lock, slide it off and away we go.<P>The clip would move a little from side to side but I couldn't get it to slide up...  upon further inspection I noticed that between the two "fingers" is another "finger" bent the other way and into a slot.<BR>No wonder I couldn't get it to slide off.<P>My question, with the limited space available, how the heck to you get that "finger" to pop out of the hole?<P>I appreciate your help.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||01-13-2000||03:51 AM||||A set of mechanics picks (look like dental tools) and a pair of lonnngg needle nose.  Look close and pick pull prod poke in the correct place in the correct order in the correct direction and in no time you'll have a complete working knowledge of several new combinations of bad words.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||01-13-2000||04:20 AM||||Not to mention the fact that blood is slippery.  You'll find this out (as I did) when the picks and/or needle nose pliers and/or anything else slips a few times.  Phaeton is right about the bad words.  This would not be a good time to invite your priest/minister/rabbi over to help.  BTW-I got a set of mechanics picks from Autozone for about $12US.||||reg||1
